    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d531">

                <pb facs="0526" n="522" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg522"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6564: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Kennedy</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-01-06T21:00:00-05:00">January 6, 1938—9
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8. The British Ambassador<anchor n="76" xml:id="d531-f1"/> has informed the
                    Department today that his Government had advised him that the Chinese Ambassador
                    in London,<anchor n="77" xml:id="d531-f2"/> in talking with the Secretary for
                    Foreign Affairs<anchor n="78" xml:id="d531-f3"/> last Monday, had said the
                    Chinese Government was negotiating with the United States Government for a loan
                    of 125 million dollars for 10 years at 3 percent and to be issued at 90; and
                    that yesterday he told the British Foreign Office that an agreement had been
                    signed in Washington for a loan of 150 million dollars, but that he could not
                    definitely say whether the United States Government were concerned or not.</p>
                <p>There is no foundation for this report as far as the American Government is
                    concerned, and the Department has no knowledge of any loan arrangement with
                    private parties. The Department is so informing Sir Ronald Lindsay.</p>
                <pb facs="0527" n="523" xml:id="pg523"/>
                <p>In his most recent talk with the Chinese Ambassador in Washington on this
                    subject, the Secretary of State, while pointing out the constant purchases of
                    Chinese silver by the American Tresaury, had made clear that any government loan
                    would require the passage of legislation.</p>
                <p>If Butterworth<anchor n="79" xml:id="d531-f4"/> should be questioned about the
                    report by the British Treasury, he may state that no such loan transaction is
                    under discussion with this Government.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
